# Overview
#
# Reports every em dash and en dash, and a double or spaced hyphen that
# stands in for one (`The job runs nightly -- the cache refills`). A
# dash joins two parts of a sentence without saying how they relate. The
# reader must work out whether the second part is a result, a contrast,
# or an aside.
#
# The message names a fix for each use of the dash. A clause is a
# subject and its verb, such as `the tests pass`. Between two clauses, a
# period or a joining word (`and`, `but`, `so`) replaces the dash.
# Commas set off an aside, and a colon follows a list-item label (`-
# **Cache**: holds the reads`). The rule sits at Required, because it
# matches the punctuation itself and each fix is mechanical.
#
# Expression
#
# Harper reads `--` as two hyphen tokens, so the double dash has two
# branches. One is two hyphens before whitespace (`nightly -- the`, and
# the last two of `---`). The other is two hyphens glued to the word
# before them (`nightly--the`).
#
# A single hyphen with whitespace and a word on both sides stands in for
# a dash too (`excluded on purpose - the`). A writer barred from the em
# dash reaches for it next. `word` matches a word by the tag that
# Harper's part-of-speech tagger gives it, such as `NOUN` (a noun) or
# `VERB` (a verb).
#
# False Positives
#
# A hyphen pair after whitespace and glued to the word after it is a
# long flag (`--commit`) and passes. Glued hyphens pass (`well-known`,
# `1-5`, `-1`), and so do list markers, which have no word before them.
# A bare `--` separator in prose fires, and the fix sets it in a code
# span.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseTypography reports the other dash characters: U+2010 to U+2012,
# U+2015, and the minus sign U+2212. It leaves the em dash and the en
# dash to this rule.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 343 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits used a dash as punctuation. Twelve of them separated a list-item
# label from its text.
expr word [NOUN, VERB, ADJ, ADV, PRON, DET, PROPN, NUM]
expr main [—, –, (-- ), (@word--), (@word - @word)]
let message "Dash. End the sentence with a period, or join the clauses with `and`, `but`, or `so`. Set an aside in commas, and put a colon after a list-item label."
let description "Flags every em dash and en dash, and a double or spaced hyphen used as one."
let becomes ""
let kind "Punctuation"
allows "The job runs nightly, and the cache refills."
allows "The flag is --commit."
allows "Run prose-lint --commit on the message."
allows "A well-known bug hides here."
allows "The range 1-5 is closed."
allows "The -1 value means unset."
allows "Pass -v to print each step."
allows "The window holds `n - 1` entries."
